Johnston Engineering is pleased to submit this proposal for mechanical engineering design of two concepts for Qcells Tech Center: a Fronloader Attachment / End Effector, and a Off Road Construction Trailer.

# Scope:

Johnston Engineering will provide mechanical engineering design services for two equipment systems under the Qcells Mobility Project: (1) a custom **Frontloader Attachment** and (2) an **Off-Road Construction Trailer**. Both systems are intended to support utility-scale solar module installation operations with improved efficiency, operator safety, and material protection.

Assumptions: 

Articulation side shift is completed using….

Rotational for side hill leveling is completed using 

Grippers 

Loader existing boom up/down 

Fine adjustment tilt 

Johnston Engineering will deliver complete 3D CAD models, 2D fabrication-ready drawings, and accompanying structural and vibrational analyses for both systems. All deliverables are targeted for completion by **February 2026** in accordance with Qcells’ project schedule. This proposal covers **design and testing support only**, excluding prototype fabrication at this stage. 

**Phase1: Frontloader Attachment Design**

Key Tasks:

- Requirements Definition (10 hours)

- Collaborate with Qcells to define final interface geometry, lifting tolerances, and operational safety constraints.

- 3D CAD Development (100 hours)

- Design modular frame, attachment interfaces, and adjustable gripping/handling features.

- Concept iteration

- Review meetings with QCells

- Structural Analysis (30 hours)

- Perform FEA to verify safe loading and stress distribution under operational conditions.

- S-N Curve based fatigue analysis on attachment end effector to validate field durability.

- Drafting Detailed Fabrication Drawings (20 hours)

- Generate 2D manufacturing drawings of complete assembly and all subcomponents.

**Phase****2: Off-Road Construction Trailer Design**

Key Tasks:  Design of simple, manually operated support leg assembly to bolt to existing trailer 

- Requirements Definition (4 hours)

- Collaborate with Qcells to define final interface geometry, lifting tolerances, and operational safety constraints.

- 3D CAD Development (16 hours)

- CAD design of trailer chassis, supports, and custom brackets for load protection.

- Structural & Load Analysis (8 Hours)

- Simulate towing loads, uneven terrain handling, and tipping under assisted lift

- Drafting Detailed Fabrication Drawings (8 hours)

- Generate 2D manufacturing drawings of complete assembly and all subcomponents.

# Cost: 

**Exclusion: **

Qcells is responsible for identifying front end loader pin location interface. 

Hardware fabrication can be completed at a later date after customer approval, but is excluded in the provided costing. 

